---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { versioned } from '../../../lib/versioned';

// Detail page for the flip bench (garage shop sheet 04). English-only, no data-i18n keys.
const planHref = '/plans/garage-shop/#s4';
const parentHref = '/projects/garage-shop';
const modelHref = '/plans/garage-shop/model/?focus=Flip%20Bench&dir=sw&state=miter&hide=Truck,Wall%20South,Wall%20East,Ceiling%20Lights,Tool%20Cabinet,Door%20North,Door%20South,Dust%20Cart,Subpanel';
const stepHref = '/plans/garage-shop/model/garage-shop-flip-bench.step';
const m = '/plans/garage-shop/model/';

const renders = [
  { src: 'render-flip-bench-sw.png', alt: 'Flip bench flat with the table saw at the end', cap: 'Bench mode: the table saw flush at the near end, both flip sections riser-up, the miter saw and planer hanging inside the frame. One flat top, 92 x 30 in at 37.75 in.' },
  { src: 'render-flip-miter-sw.png', alt: 'Flip bench with the miter saw up', cap: 'Miter station: the middle section flipped, its table level with the saw section on one side and the planer section\'s riser on the other, 24 and 30 in of wing.' },
  { src: 'render-flip-planer-sw.png', alt: 'Flip bench with the planer up', cap: 'Planing: the end section flipped, the flat miter section is the infeed, boards run along the bench and out past the end. Bed 1.5 in above the bench.' },
  { src: 'render-flip-both-ne.png', alt: 'Flip bench with both tools up, from behind', cap: 'Both up, from the back: the plunger knob on the end panel, the risers hanging with their concrete, the apron along the back of the frame.' },
  { src: 'render-flip-end.png', alt: 'End view of the flip bench on the shaft line', cap: 'End-on through the miter section, saw up: the shaft at 30.5 in, the 4 in box centred on it, the riser hanging below.' },
  { src: 'render-flip-side.png', alt: 'Side view of the flip bench with both tools up', cap: 'Side, both up: 92 in on six 3 in casters, four 29 in panels, the saw bay with its two shelves, a box in each flip bay with its bearings on the panel faces.' },
  { src: 'render-flip-top.png', alt: 'Flip bench from above', cap: 'From above: the saw with its fence rails across the bench, the miter box and the planer box between the panels, the plunger on the outer panel of each.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="flip bench &middot; garage shop &middot; rt-541.io" description="Flip bench for the garage shop: the DWE7491 table saw sunk flush at one end, the DCS785 miter saw on a flip section in the middle, the DW735 planer on a flip section at the other end. Renders, numbers, STEP.">
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-8">
    <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-eyebrow uppercase text-chrome opacity-85 mb-5 pb-3 border-b border-chrome/20">
      <a href="/projects" class="no-underline hover:underline">projects</a> / <a href={parentHref} class="no-underline hover:underline">garage shop</a> / flip bench
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-[34px] leading-[1.1] tracking-display text-chrome mb-3">Flip bench</h1>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-ice leading-[1.65] max-w-[58ch]">
      One 92 x 30 in rolling bench for the three machines. The DWE7491 table saw stands in the end bay on a platform
      that puts its table flush with the bench top. The DCS785 miter saw rides a 4 in torsion box that flips in the
      middle bay, the DW735 planer another in the far bay; each box has its tool on one face and a 4.5 in riser bench
      top on the other. Both tools down: a flat bench and 60 in of outfeed for the saw. Miter up: its table is at
      bench height with a wing either side. Planer up: the bench is its infeed. Index plungers and draw latches lock
      each section, and concrete cast into the risers puts each section's centre of gravity on its shaft.
    </p>
    <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 mt-5 list-none p-0 font-mono text-[11px] tracking-meta uppercase">
      <li><a href={planHref} class="text-chrome no-underline hover:underline">sheet 04 in the drawing set &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><a href={modelHref} class="text-chrome no-underline hover:underline">open in the 3d viewer &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><a href={stepHref} class="text-chrome no-underline hover:underline">step file &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-6">
    <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-eyebrow uppercase text-chrome opacity-70 mb-4">// renders</p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      {renders.map(r => (
        <figure class="m-0">
          <img src={versioned(m + r.src)} alt={r.alt} loading="lazy" class="w-full h-auto rounded border border-chrome/20" />
          <figcaption class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-meta text-subtle mt-2">{r.cap}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-meta text-subtle mt-4">The tools are drawn as their bounding boxes: DWE7491 22.7 x 29.2 x 13 in, DCS785 24.5 x 31 x 18.5 in, DW735 22 x 24 x 20 in. The viewer's states are "Closed up" (flat), "Miter station", "Planing" and "Working" (both up).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12">
    <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-eyebrow uppercase text-chrome opacity-70 mb-4">// by the numbers</p>
    <dl class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 m-0">
      <div class="border-l border-chrome/20 pl-3"><dt class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-meta uppercase text-subtle">bench</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[18px] font-serif text-chrome">92 x 30 x 37.75 in</dd></div>
      <div class="border-l border-chrome/20 pl-3"><dt class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-meta uppercase text-subtle">bays</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[18px] font-serif text-chrome">24 saw, 32 miter, 30 planer</dd></div>
      <div class="border-l border-chrome/20 pl-3"><dt class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-meta uppercase text-subtle">shafts</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[18px] font-serif text-chrome">two, 1 in, 30.5 in AFF</dd></div>
      <div class="border-l border-chrome/20 pl-3"><dt class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-meta uppercase text-subtle">planer bed</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[18px] font-serif text-chrome">39.25 in</dd></div>
      <div class="border-l border-chrome/20 pl-3"><dt class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-meta uppercase text-subtle">swing clearance</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[18px] font-serif text-chrome">1.3 in at the rails</dd></div>
      <div class="border-l border-chrome/20 pl-3"><dt class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-meta uppercase text-subtle">ballast</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[18px] font-serif text-chrome">63 + 146 lb concrete</dd></div>
      <div class="border-l border-chrome/20 pl-3"><dt class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-meta uppercase text-subtle">residual</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[18px] font-serif text-chrome">4 lb / 14 lb</dd></div>
      <div class="border-l border-chrome/20 pl-3"><dt class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-meta uppercase text-subtle">on the casters</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[18px] font-serif text-chrome">~720 lb, six 3 in</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12 pb-20">
    <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-eyebrow uppercase text-chrome opacity-70 mb-2">// what makes it work</p>
    <div class="prose-content text-[14px]">
      <p>
        The riser is the idea that makes one bench out of three machines. A flip section with a tool on one face and a
        flat face on the other would leave the flat face 4.5 in below the miter saw's table, so the sections either
        side could never be its wings. Putting a 4.5 in tall box on the free face fixes that: in bench mode the riser
        top is the bench top, and when the miter comes up its table lands exactly where the riser tops of the sections
        beside it are. The table saw gets the same treatment from the other direction, sunk on a platform until its
        table is flush, with the blade running along the bench so the flat sections are its outfeed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The riser is also where the ballast goes. Each section has to balance about its own shaft: the miter saw pulls
        546 lb-in and the planer 1,081, so concrete is cast into the risers, 1 in under the miter riser's top (63 lb)
        and 2.5 in under the planer's (146 lb). That leaves 4 lb and 14 lb at the bench edge, on the tool side of
        neutral so a section never runs away, and the last of it is settled at the balance test with the real tools.
        Steel plate does the same job at four times the price.
      </p>
      <p>
        The swing sets the height. The miter saw's corner sweeps a 26.3 in radius and has to pass the 2x4 side rails
        of the frame, which puts the shaft at 30.5 in with 3 in casters and the bench top at 37.75. Two-inch casters
        bring it down an inch; there is no other way down without notching the frame. Each box is a 4 in torsion box
        open along both long sides, carried by a flanged shaft collar at each end plate on its own 1 in shaft in
        UCFL205-16 flange bearings mounted on the panel faces inside the bay, so panel B carries a bearing on each face
        and neither shaft passes through it. The tool bolts to a 3/4 plate that fastens to the box with six 1/4-20
        bolts into T-nuts.
      </p>
      <p>
        The lock is positive in both directions: a 1/2 in spring index plunger through a steel bushing in the outer
        panel of each section drops into one of two bushings in the box's end plate, 10 in off the shaft line, so it
        indexes at 0 and 180 and snaps home by itself; a draw latch on the shared middle panel pulls the box tight so
        there is no play. The bench parks under the tool cabinet in bench mode with 10 in to spare and rolls out
        before any tool flips up. Printed on the Core One: plunger keepers, a holster for the latch keepers, T-nut
        alignment jigs, a dust hood adapter for the miter saw and a zero-clearance insert blank for the table saw.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
